// LeetCode 279 bench kernel — the DP, at a size where the table is the point.
//
// WHICH SOLVER TO BENCH IS ITSELF THE INTERESTING CHOICE. The closed form is
// O(√n) and answers n = 10^7 in microseconds; benching it would measure process
// startup. The DP is O(n√n) and builds a table of n entries, so it is the one
// with real work in it — and it is also the one anyone actually writes, since
// the closed form requires knowing two theorems.
//
// SO THE WORKLOAD IS ONE BIG TABLE, NOT MANY SMALL QUERIES. A single
// `least_squares(n)` at n = 300,000 does ~300k·√300k/2 ≈ 82 million inner steps.
// That is the shape the DP is for: the table is built once and every entry is
// used by later entries, so there is nothing to hoist out and nothing the
// optimizer can erase — each `least[i]` genuinely depends on up to √i earlier
// ones.
//
// PARALLEL LANE: NONE, AND THAT IS THE HONEST ANSWER. The DP is strictly
// sequential — `least[i]` reads `least[i - j*j]` for j up to √i, so every entry
// depends on earlier ones and no prefix can be computed independently. Splitting
// it would require a different algorithm, not an annotation. Katas 276 and 277
// fan out over independent instances because they HAVE independent instances;
// this one does not, and adding a fake outer loop over repeated identical
// queries would manufacture parallelism the problem does not contain.
//
// The sink is `least[n]` plus a checksum over the table, so a lane that computed
// only the last entry — or skipped entries — would be caught.

fn least_squares_with_checksum(n: i64) -> i64 {
    let mut least: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    least.push(0);
    let mut i = 1;
    while i <= n {
        let mut best = i;
        let mut j = 1;
        while j * j <= i {
            let cand = least[i - j * j] + 1;
            if cand < best { best = cand; }
            j = j + 1;
        }
        least.push(best);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    // checksum every entry, so the whole table has to be right
    let mut sum = 0;
    let mut k = 0;
    while k <= n {
        sum = (sum * 31 + least[k]) % 1000000007;
        k = k + 1;
    }
    return (sum * 10 + least[n]) % 1000000007;
}

fn main() {
    println(least_squares_with_checksum(300000));
}
